he belt and pulley using the matched tensioner procedure.","check":"The work can proceed safely without improvising a missing holding or access tool."},{"title":"Extract the old pump squarely","body":"Match the replacement and seal to the actual housing. Remove the four pump retaining nuts. Thread two matching M6 bolts into the opposed extraction ears shown in Fig. 13 and advance them evenly until the pump releases. Stop for damaged threads or an uneven release. Keep tools clear of the mating bore.","check":"The pump comes out squarely and the studs and sealing bore are undamaged."},{"title":"Fit the new seal and tighten evenly","body":"Clean the mating bore, fit a new seal and use only a seal-compatible assembly lubricant specified for the installed pump. Locate the pump squarely over the studs and tighten the four nuts alternately and evenly. Bentley lists 10 Nm for the factory pump-to-cover nuts and 10 Nm for the pump pulley fasteners. Confirm these values for the actual hardware.","check":"The seal is seated without pinching, the pump is flush and the applied torques are recorded."},{"title":"Restore the cooling system and verify","body":"Restore the pulley, belt, hoses, fan and shroud, then check routing and clearance. Follow the verified fill and bleed procedure for the installed cooling system before running the engine. Include a leak check, controlled temperature check and a final cold-level recheck. Stop for an unknown cap or bleed sequence, leakage or abnormal temperature.","check":"The job checklist includes completion verification rather than ending when the new part is installed."}]}'::jsonb, source_locator='170-8–170-9, Figs. 12–13; 170-12; 020-13'
